{"schema":1,"fm":{"schema":1,"id":"trial-preparation-track","kind":"workflow","title":"Trial preparation track","description":"Takes a case from case theory to trial notebook in gated steps, mapping each element to proof, planning witnesses and exhibits, listing pretrial motions and building the trial-day binder.","category":"legal-practice","version":"1.0.0","status":"incubating","stage":["plan","build"],"role":["legal-professional"],"subject":["law"],"requires":["none"],"inputs":["notes","document","text"],"output":["plan","table","checklist","questions"],"risk":"read-only","advice_risk":["legal"],"invocation":"user","effort":"deep","interaction":"interactive","model_tier":"frontier","reasoning":"recommended","level":"expert","tags":["trial-preparation","case-theory","proof-chart","trial-notebook","motions-in-limine"],"pairs_with":{"prompts":["prepare-cross-examination-outline","draft-opening-statement","draft-jury-instructions","build-litigation-deadline-calendar"],"personas":["paralegal"]},"args":[{"name":"case_summary","description":"The claims and defences, who you act for, the key facts, witnesses, documents, and what the other side says.","type":"text","required":true},{"name":"trial_details","description":"Court and jurisdiction, jury or judge alone, trial date, the pretrial order or deadlines, and any time limits.","type":"text","required":true}],"steps":[{"id":"case-theory","file":"steps/01-case-theory.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","artifact":"trial-prep/01-case-theory.md"},{"id":"proof-map","file":"steps/02-proof-map.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","artifact":"trial-prep/02-proof-map.md"},{"id":"witness-plan","file":"steps/03-witness-plan.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","artifact":"trial-prep/03-witness-plan.md"},{"id":"exhibits-motions","file":"steps/04-exhibits-and-motions.md","stage":"build","gate":"approve","artifact":"trial-prep/04-exhibits-motions.md"},{"id":"trial-notebook","file":"steps/05-trial-notebook.md","stage":"build","gate":"none","artifact":"trial-prep/05-trial-notebook.md"}],"authorship":"ai-assisted","authors":["gabrielanhaia"],"last_reviewed":"2026-10-11","changelog":[{"version":"1.0.0","note":"First version."}]},"body":"Prepares a case for trial the way an experienced trial team does: settle the theory first, prove every element with admissible evidence, plan each witness, deal with exhibits and pretrial motions, and finish with a trial notebook. Each step writes one artifact and stops for the attorney's approval, because gaps found in the proof map often change the theory or the witness list.\n\n<case_summary>\n{{case_summary}}\n</case_summary>\n\n<trial_details>\n{{trial_details}}\n</trial_details>\n\n- You give general information, not professional advice. You are not a doctor, therapist, lawyer, accountant or financial adviser, and you do not replace one.\n- Say so once, briefly, near the start: what you can help with here and what needs a qualified professional.\n- Do not diagnose, prescribe, give dosages, predict a legal outcome, or recommend a specific investment, tax position or legal action for this person.\n- When the situation is serious, urgent, high-stakes or specific to their circumstances, say which kind of professional to see and what to bring to that appointment.\n- If anything suggests immediate danger to health or safety, tell them to contact local emergency services now, before anything else.\n- Rules, prices and laws differ by country and change over time. Name the assumption you are making and tell them to check it locally.\n\nRules for every step:\n- Use only the facts, documents and rules supplied. Mark anything needed but missing [NEEDED] and never invent evidence, testimony, rules or authorities.\n- Deadlines, evidence rules and procedure differ by court; mark each to verify against the pretrial order and local rules.\n- Present weaknesses as fully as strengths.\n- Everything is a draft for the responsible attorney.\n- End each artifact with open questions and what to gather before the next step.","steps":[{"id":"case-theory","file":"steps/01-case-theory.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","artifact":"trial-prep/01-case-theory.md","text":"# Step 1: Case theory\n\n1. State the case theory in three sentences: what happened, why the law entitles the client to win, and why the fact-finder should want that result.\n2. Propose a theme of a few words that fits the evidence.\n3. List the other side's likely theory and its best facts.\n4. List the bad facts for the client and how the theory accounts for each.\n5. Note any decisions the attorney must make now (for example claims to drop, stipulations to seek).\n\nSections: Theory, Theme, Their theory, Bad facts, Decisions, Questions. Stop for approval."},{"id":"proof-map","file":"steps/02-proof-map.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","artifact":"trial-prep/02-proof-map.md","text":"# Step 2: Proof map\n\n1. Break every claim and defence into elements, using elements the attorney supplies or marking them [NEEDED].\n2. For each element, list the witnesses and exhibits that prove or rebut it, and any admissibility issue (foundation, hearsay, authentication, privilege) to resolve.\n3. Mark elements with thin or no proof and what could close the gap (a witness, a records custodian, a stipulation, a request for admission).\n\nSections: Proof map (table: Element | Our proof | Their proof | Admissibility issues | Gap), Gaps to close, Questions. Stop for approval."},{"id":"witness-plan","file":"steps/03-witness-plan.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","artifact":"trial-prep/03-witness-plan.md","text":"# Step 3: Witness plan\n\n1. Order the witnesses to tell the story clearly, with the elements each one proves.\n2. For each witness: direct examination topics or cross-examination goals, exhibits to use, impeachment material, preparation sessions needed, and subpoena or travel needs.\n3. Expert witnesses: opinions to be given, disclosure status, and challenges expected.\n4. Estimate time per witness against any time limit.\n\nSections: Witness order (table), Witness notes, Experts, Timing, Questions. Stop for approval."},{"id":"exhibits-motions","file":"steps/04-exhibits-and-motions.md","stage":"build","gate":"approve","artifact":"trial-prep/04-exhibits-motions.md","text":"# Step 4: Exhibits and pretrial motions\n\n1. Exhibit list: each exhibit with its number, description, sponsoring witness, foundation needed, likely objection and whether a stipulation could resolve it.\n2. Motions to consider: motions in limine to exclude or admit evidence, and other pretrial matters, each with the evidence affected and the argument in one line, marked for attorney decision.\n3. Pretrial filings checklist from the pretrial order, with dates to verify.\n\nSections: Exhibit list, Motions to consider, Pretrial filings, Questions. Stop for approval."},{"id":"trial-notebook","file":"steps/05-trial-notebook.md","stage":"build","gate":"none","artifact":"trial-prep/05-trial-notebook.md","text":"# Step 5: Trial notebook\n\nAssemble the trial-day binder plan:\n1. Contents with tabs: case theory and theme, proof map, opening outline, witness outlines in order, exhibit list with status, proposed instructions or legal framework, motion rulings, closing outline linked to the proof map.\n2. A daily checklist: witnesses confirmed, exhibits copied, equipment, objections log.\n3. A closing outline that walks the fact-finder through each element with the evidence that proved it, to update as trial goes on.\n\nSections: Notebook contents, Daily checklist, Closing outline, Open items."}]}
